WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.960
- She's an Oscar and Golden
Globe nominated actress.

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.790
She's got "Modern Love" season two

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:06.980
on Amazon Prime video coming soon.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.190
She'll be starring in the new
"Cinderella" movie in July

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:13.600
and she's got a new podcast
called "Minnie Questions."

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.790
You can check it out wherever
you get your podcasts.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:19.205
Give it up for Minnie Driver everybody.

00:00:19.205 --> 00:00:22.986
(audience cheering)

00:00:22.986 --> 00:00:23.819
Hello.

00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:26.406
- Hello.

00:00:26.406 --> 00:00:28.470
- I am such a big fan.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.090
Oh my God I'm so excited
that you're doing my show

00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.420
but I gotta ask you, how
about that officer? 91.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.370
He's still like, whatever I'm doing it.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.560
- Thank you for your service, sir.

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:41.710
You are amazing.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:42.820
I'm very, very happy

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.854
that you have a lovely lady in your life.

00:00:44.854 --> 00:00:46.078
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:46.911
- I know-

00:00:46.911 --> 00:00:49.272
lady friend was my
favorite term of the day.

00:00:49.272 --> 00:00:51.470
(Kelly chuckles)

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:53.970
So, what's one thing that
you're so passionate about

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.850
that you think you'll do into your 90s?

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.850
- You know, I hope that I am
still surfing when I'm 90.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.700
I am an almost a lifelong lover of surfing

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.170
and a lot of my friends that I've surfed

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.743
where I've lived in Malibu
for the last 25 years.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:17.510
I surf with a lot of older people

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.300
and they taught me everything I know.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.810
And I watched them stay

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.460
in shape and stay healthy
and happy and vibing.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:28.141
And so I hope I'm still
doing that when I'm 91.

00:01:28.141 --> 00:01:31.313
- What's been one of your
wildest surfing experiences?

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.930
- Well, I lived for a little while

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.400
on the North shore of Oahu
and I think quite mistakenly

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.823
go out surfing by myself, which, you know,

00:01:43.823 --> 00:01:47.730
they're big, powerful waves
and this big, powerful swells

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:48.918
but you know,

00:01:48.918 --> 00:01:52.803
it teaches you to be respectful
and you watch and you learn.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.700
I went out and it was a great day.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.730
But as you know, with a
lot of breaks in Hawaii

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.300
out of nowhere, I suddenly saw the horizon

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.190
start to kind of bubble a little bit.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.690
And this really...

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:08.710
It's, what's called the clean up set.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:13.710
This giant outside set was
coming in and I just froze.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.010
I had no idea what to do.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.730
There's the wave was about, you know

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.420
it was like a 15 foot face.

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:22.750
It was a big wave for me.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.730
And there was this
female surfer next to me.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:26.857
And she was like,

00:02:26.857 --> 00:02:29.730
"You know, you better
choose where you gonna go?

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:33.030
Are you gonna try to take the wave?

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:33.863
'Cause if you're not,

00:02:33.863 --> 00:02:37.420
you need to paddle really
hard to get up over the peak."

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.070
So I started harder than I've ever.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.610
It was like going...

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:42.510
It was like...

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.630
I felt like I was paddling vertically.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.940
It was like in all of
those, those movies where-

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.560
- That's what I was just thinking of.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.030
Like I was thinking of
when you're on a boat,

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.157
you know those movies when
you're on a boat in a movie

00:02:53.157 --> 00:02:54.860
and all this like perfect
storm it's like that

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:58.100
and you're like going straight
up and I'm like, "No."

00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:58.933
Yeah, and then all of a sudden

00:02:58.933 --> 00:02:59.967
it comes across and you're like,

00:02:59.967 --> 00:03:02.030
"They're gonna die.
How are they not dead?"

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.950
Like, it's like, I'm
not an adventure seeker.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:05.783
Is your son?

00:03:05.783 --> 00:03:07.530
Is he an adventure seeker?

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.880
- Yeah, he can't help himself.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:10.713
He's...

00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:13.110
Oh, he's so cute.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.030
He's a big strong guy and

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.040
he's really active and he's always playing

00:03:19.321 --> 00:03:20.450
he knows like a little bull.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.336
He hasn't figured out how-

00:03:22.336 --> 00:03:25.280
He hasn't figured out
his own strength yet.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.392
- I'm so fearful that that will be my son.

00:03:28.392 --> 00:03:30.730
He's obsessed with Peter Pan

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.480
I'm like, "You can't fly,
please don't try and fly."

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.680
Like he just jumps off of everything,

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.750
he's really into Peter Pan.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.820
I feel like I'm gonna
live at the ER one day.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:40.950
It's fine.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.220
What's your relationship
with your son like?

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:44.810
Is it y'all have a pretty strong bond?

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:45.643
- Yeah.

00:03:45.643 --> 00:03:48.570
You know, as a single mom raising him

00:03:49.766 --> 00:03:54.037
and he's a very emotionally
articulate person.

00:03:55.036 --> 00:03:56.260
He's amazing.

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:59.260
He is kind and he is considerate

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:01.910
and he makes...

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.180
He kind of makes friends wherever he goes.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.570
He's like, I often think of

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.822
he's like one of those,
you know, in Snow White's

00:04:08.822 --> 00:04:10.422
going through the movie and like

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.130
the flower petals and the birds
are kind of all around her,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.530
Henry kind of crumps his way

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.480
through life with flower petals,

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.420
and sunbeams coming all around
him, 'cause he's a good guy.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:23.520
- What a beautiful thing.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.850
I love the visual.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:27.330
Your new podcast is
called "Minnie Questions"

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.370
so what's it about?

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:31.390
- Well, I really like...

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.750
I really love asking people questions

00:04:34.750 --> 00:04:39.030
and I love to hear how
different their answers are.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.010
And I used to do it
when I was a little kid,

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.320
I would interview people.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:45.000
I would ask the same
questions to family members

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.030
and I've always loved the form of that.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:48.870
So the concept was,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.840
what if I asked the same seven questions

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.640
to a bunch of really
cool, interesting people

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.850
and maybe we could create some
kind of cultural anthology

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:03.780
to understand what a
scientist's last meal,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.320
that dream last meal would be

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.070
or the musician or the actor

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.530
or the author

00:05:10.749 --> 00:05:13.880
to help us feel more connected

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.290
and also see how it individually.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.340
- Here's a question.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.820
If you could switch
places with one person,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:21.653
you live your life.

00:05:21.653 --> 00:05:22.486
There's nothing wrong with you.

00:05:22.486 --> 00:05:23.319
You're great.

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:24.270
But if you could switch places

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.483
with one person who would it be?

00:05:27.473 --> 00:05:29.443
- Oh my goodness.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:33.750
I would switch places with

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:36.194
with Elon Musk and I would do-

00:05:36.194 --> 00:05:37.027
- Oh my God!

00:05:37.027 --> 00:05:38.568
That's mine.

00:05:38.568 --> 00:05:39.401
That is...

00:05:39.401 --> 00:05:40.234
I am obsessed.

00:05:40.234 --> 00:05:41.130
I've read like books on him.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:41.963
I'm obsessed.

00:05:41.963 --> 00:05:44.440
I think he's so cool and that's mine.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.273
- Me too.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.470
- [Kelly] I think he's so interesting.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:49.483
I want my brain to work like that.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:51.660
- I want my brain to work like that

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.400
but I also want like
still part of my brain

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:57.710
that would spend money in an incredibly

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:01.260
like different, creative way than perhaps

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.595
amazing, brilliant Elon
Musk would spend it.

00:06:03.595 --> 00:06:06.990
And I would still any spend, like you know

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:08.850
a billion in a day probably.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:09.683
- Yeah, I know.

00:06:09.683 --> 00:06:11.170
Isn't it just rough?

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.170
(Kelly chuckles)

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:13.630
No, I think it's amazing too.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:16.190
All the things like innovative
weird kind of things,

00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:17.300
you know, he'll come
out with something like

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.720
kind of strange and like what?

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:20.500
But I think all those
weird things will lead...

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:22.110
They always lead to something amazing.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.980
Like, you know, I'm so excited,

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.730
about what they're doing with space

00:06:27.975 --> 00:06:29.143
and how he talks about,

00:06:30.032 --> 00:06:31.380
AI and like all that stuff.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:32.220
I think it's so interesting.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:33.810
It's so funny you picked him.

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:35.160
That would be who I'd pick.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.550
- That is funny 'cause it's like,

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:40.290
he's been on my mind and I did...

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:41.800
Well, I actually haven't
read a bunch of books

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:42.877
but I started reading a book about him

00:06:42.877 --> 00:06:44.730
and it is fascinating.

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:46.780
And it's really funny that
you picked him as well.

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.863
See, now you've got to come on my show.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.110
- Well, you know, I'll give
honest, raw answers ma'am

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:56.347
so (Kelly chuckles) my mom is like,

00:06:56.347 --> 00:06:58.450
"You could use a tiny bit of a filter"

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.568
and I'm like, "Why?"

00:07:00.568 --> 00:07:02.820
- I don't think so.

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:03.670
I think it's good.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:07.280
I think filterless is good.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.430
- Is it true that you
had a hard time dealing

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:12.170
with this sudden huge fame after?

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:13.777
And I want to ask this
question 'cause after

00:07:13.777 --> 00:07:15.470
"Goodwill Hunting," which I love.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:18.180
I love everything you
do..."Return to Me"...all of them.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:21.630
But my question is because I
feel like it was really hard.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.880
Like mine was like 180 overnight-like fame

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:26.650
and I never really wanted fame.

00:07:26.650 --> 00:07:28.350
I know that seems like
such a cliche answer

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.380
that famous people say, but I didn't.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.690
I just really wanted to
be a background singer

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:34.270
and travel the world for
all these different singers

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:35.370
and that was my big dream.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:38.190
And so anyway, my life kind of took a turn

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.640
and I felt like it was a lot at once

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.060
and I couldn't go to the store.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:43.800
I couldn't like all of
a sudden it was like

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:45.110
I can't take it back.

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.450
I had this like complete meltdown

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:47.580
in my garage with my brother.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.440
And I was like, "I can't take it back."

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:50.570
Like, I'm just, it's done.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:51.470
Like, I'm just, it's out there.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.590
I can never have a normal job
to where people won't know.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:56.430
And it's like, and it
kind of freaked me out.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.263
I'm totally fine now.

00:07:57.263 --> 00:07:58.960
But I just mean like I freaked out.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.720
Did you ever have that like right after,

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.733
cause that film was so huge.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:05.783
- Oh yeah.

00:08:05.783 --> 00:08:08.420
Yeah. It was very, very weird

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.210
because there's no one

00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:12.710
except your family and maybe

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:13.843
a couple of your closest friends

00:08:13.843 --> 00:08:16.900
who you could ever complain to about that.

00:08:16.900 --> 00:08:18.960
Because from the outside,
it really is like

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.170
you've you found the pot
at the end of the rainbow

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:26.183
the pot of gold. You you've
got, you got everything.

00:08:26.183 --> 00:08:31.183
And yet with it comes this
you, the way that you are then

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:36.030
processed the way that your
image, the way that people think

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:39.600
that you are, everything is metabolized

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.024
by all these other people.

00:08:42.024 --> 00:08:44.790
And it's very easy to lose
sight of who you actually are

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:47.010
because you, you start
seeing yourself reflected

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.070
in all the eyes of all these
people and it's overwhelming

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.470
and weird and very, very, very strange.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:55.730
And it's kind of like standing
on the top of a, a big cliff.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:57.380
And then free-falling.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:59.150
- The advice I give any
time anybody's like,

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:00.120
Oh, what advice would you give?

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.400
Because they're like a decade younger

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:02.520
than me or whatever in the business.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.460
I'm like, don't read anything.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.475
Like, you know, cause you're
either going to get a big ego

00:09:06.475 --> 00:09:08.720
or you're going to want
to crawl in and hole die.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:10.690
So neither are great.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:12.333
So yeah.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.670
- Yeah. And there was a lot of

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.490
certainly like in the nineties,
like there was no recourse

00:09:18.490 --> 00:09:19.860
like stuff that was written about you.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.530
There was no social
media to, to combat any

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:26.850
of those opinions that were
offered up by, you know

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:30.830
armchair critics or just
journalists who felt

00:09:30.830 --> 00:09:32.070
like having fun with the new kid

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:35.230
in town or they just didn't
like you or whatever it was.

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:36.650
So there was, there was

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.430
there was nowhere to put any of that.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.550
And for all the terribleness

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:44.350
of the internet that we all
know exists with trolling

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:48.630
and the, the, the nightmare
of, of that whole world

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:53.480
I kind of love being able
to have my voice be clear

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:55.830
and people can find what it is

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.030
I think about something.

00:09:57.908 --> 00:09:59.100
And I can disseminate some rumor

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.360
before it's even begun or I
can address something head

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:07.360
on in my own words, calmly
clearly and, and, and nip stuff

00:10:07.858 --> 00:10:12.858
in the bud, like stop it
growing into, into bad stuff.

00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:14.520
- Absolutely. I agree.

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:17.070
My career started out and
all that stuff didn't exist.

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:18.000
All the social media.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.020
And then literally like a
few years into my career

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:21.820
it started and I was like, Oh

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:23.710
it was such a blessing
to be able to go to just

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.400
kind of attack the rumor mill
and say, that's not real.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:27.233
This isn't true.

00:10:27.233 --> 00:10:28.066
That's not what I said.

00:10:28.066 --> 00:10:30.190
Like, it's a really
beautiful thing to wait to

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:31.670
get to actually clear things up.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:34.000
But I love talking with you.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.410
I can't wait to do your podcast one day

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:36.840
once I've really thought
about my questions.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:37.673
Or maybe I shouldn't think

00:10:37.673 --> 00:10:38.834
and just say what comes to my mind

00:10:38.834 --> 00:10:43.800
but everybody, Minnie's podcast
is called Minnie Questions

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:45.360
and they are great questions.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.780
So you should check it out
wherever you get your podcasts.

00:10:47.780 --> 00:10:49.130
You should listen to that.

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:50.170
Also look for Minnie

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.847
in "Modern Love," season
two on Amazon prime

00:10:52.847 --> 00:10:55.430
and in the new "Cinderella"
movie this summer.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:56.993
She's doing everything y'all.

